\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Multi-turn attention degradation.}
Recent work has established that LLMs systematically degrade across conversation turns. \citet{laban2025lost} showed that model performance drops on tasks placed in later turns, even when the tasks are identical to earlier ones. \citet{liu2026intent} traced a related failure to intent mismatch, where models progressively shift from the user's original intent as dialogue extends. MT-Bench-101 \citep{bai2024mtbench101} provides fine-grained multi-turn evaluation across 1388 turn-level instances, revealing that capability varies substantially across task categories. \citet{dongre2026attention} established the quantitative foundation for this line of work: they defined the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) and showed that attention to system-prompt goal tokens decays by 27--48\% across turns in 12 models, while residual stream probes retain goal information with AUC\,=\,0.98. We observe a steeper 70.8\% decline on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct (\S\ref{sec:experiments}). This dissociation between attention collapse and residual persistence motivates our gating intervention. Their work characterizes the phenomenon; ours attempts the first systematic cure and, in doing so, uncovers why lightweight interventions fail to transfer from training to inference.

\paragraph{Instruction stability.}
\citet{li2024measuring} independently quantified instruction instability in multi-turn dialogs and proposed split-softmax---separating instruction from conversation tokens during attention computation---as an inference-time remedy. Split-softmax restructures the attention mechanism itself and requires no retraining, but cannot address exposure bias in auxiliary losses that depend on training-time optimization. Our work adds systematic diagnosis (five-way controlled comparison + PCGrad exclusion), exposure bias attribution, and training-time mitigation via scheduled sampling; our $\beta$-steering complements split-softmax as an alternative inference-time approach (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\paragraph{Residual gating and adaptive depth.}
ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero} showed that residual scaling shapes optimization dynamics. Subsequent work targets computational efficiency: LayerDrop \citep{fan2020layerdrop}, Mixture-of-Depths \citep{raposo2024mixture}, FlexiDepth \citep{luo2025flexidepth}, and CALM \citep{schuster2022calm}. ThreadGate applies residual gating to a different objective: recovering attention patterns in multi-turn dialogue.

\paragraph{Attention manipulation.}
StreamingLLM \citep{xiao2024streaming} identified attention sinks and proposed anchor token retention; \citet{hsieh2024found} showed positional bias against middle-positioned information; \citet{qiu2025gated} replaced softmax with a learned gate for sparse attention. These approaches intervene inside the attention mechanism, while ThreadGate operates on the residual stream. Our results suggest that neither pathway alone overcomes the distributional gap between teacher-forced training and autoregressive inference.

\paragraph{Multi-task gradient conflict.}
GradNorm \citep{chen2018gradnorm}, PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, CAGrad \citep{liu2021cagrad}, and Nash-MTL \citep{navon2022nash} treat gradient conflict between competing losses as a root cause to be resolved through better optimization. We repurpose PCGrad as a diagnostic tool: eliminating directional conflict between NTP and GAR gradients does not improve GAR convergence. Gradient conflict is a symptom rather than the cause.

\paragraph{Exposure bias.}
Teacher forcing creates a distributional mismatch between training and inference that compounds over sequence length \citep{wiseman2016sequence,arora2022exposure}. Scheduled Sampling \citep{bengio2015scheduled} introduced curriculum-based mixing of ground-truth and model-generated tokens. MIXER \citep{ranzato2016sequence} combined token-level and sequence-level objectives, and Professor Forcing \citep{lamb2016professor} used adversarial regularization to align hidden state distributions under both regimes. \citet{he2019exposure} showed that autoregressive models exhibit self-recovery from exposure bias at the token prediction level. SEARNN \citep{leblond2018searnn} proposed locally recurrent search-based training to avoid the mismatch entirely. This literature focuses on generation quality degradation in primary objectives. No prior work examines how exposure bias affects auxiliary objectives that target attention distributions, nor how it manifests in multi-turn dialogue settings. Our work identifies a distinct failure mode: auxiliary attention losses that converge under teacher forcing fail completely under autoregressive generation, even when NTP remains robust, because token-level self-recovery does not extend to distributional properties of attention.

\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{Goal Accessibility Ratio.}
We denote the task specification tokens in the system prompt as \emph{goal tokens} $\mathcal{G}$. \citet{dongre2026attention} defined the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) for a model with $L$ layers and $H$ heads at turn $\tau$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar}
  \text{GAR}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H \cdot |\mathcal{R}_\tau|} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{R}_\tau} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ is the set of response tokens at turn $\tau$ and $A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)}$ is the attention weight from token $i$ to token $j$ at layer $\ell$, head $h$.

\paragraph{Two GAR variants.}
\textbf{GAR-Dongre} is the original definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:gar}), averaging over all response tokens. \textbf{GAR-ours} restricts the measurement to the \emph{last token} of each assistant turn:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar-ours}
  \text{GAR\text{-}ours}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{t_\tau,\, j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $t_\tau$ is the last token index at turn $\tau$. Attention at this position reflects the information drawn upon for continuation; we use GAR-ours as the optimization target for $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}).

\subsection{ThreadGate Architecture}

Inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}, ThreadGate modulates the residual stream with a content-and-turn-conditioned gate at each transformer layer. The gated residual update at layer $l$ is
\begin{equation}
  x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l),
\end{equation}
where $f_l$ denotes the sublayer computation (self-attention or FFN). The gate $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer MLP:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gate}
  g_l = \sigma\!\bigl(W_2\,\text{ReLU}(W_1\,[\text{proj}(x_l);\;\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)] + b_1) + b_2\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\text{proj}(\cdot)$ projects from the hidden dimension to 16 units and $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ is a 16-dimensional turn embedding encoding turn distance $t_d$ (turns since system prompt) and turn index $t_i$ (absolute position) via learned lookup tables. The concatenated 32-dimensional input passes through a 64-unit hidden layer to produce a scalar gate. Each of the 28 layers in Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct receives its own gate network. Gates are initialized near 0.82 to preserve pretrained computation, with a 10$\times$ learning rate multiplier. For the Uniform Gate ablation, $\text{emb}(\cdot)$ is replaced by a learnable constant vector, preserving gate capacity while removing turn awareness.

\subsection{Training Objective}\label{sec:loss}

The training objective combines three terms:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \lambda_\text{GAR}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} + \lambda_\text{div}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{div}.
\end{equation}
$\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP}$ is the standard next-token prediction loss. The GAR loss targets a fixed attention level to goal tokens:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} = \text{MSE}(\text{GAR}, \tau),
\end{equation}
where $\tau = 0.5$. GAR is measured at the last token of each assistant turn (GAR-ours, \S\ref{sec:background}). The gate diversity loss prevents collapse to a uniform value:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{div} = \text{ReLU}(\sigma_\text{min} - \text{std}(\mathbf{g})),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{g} = [g_1, \ldots, g_L]$ and $\sigma_\text{min}$ is a minimum standard deviation threshold. Both $\lambda_\text{GAR} = \lambda_\text{div} = 1.0$. We additionally tested three alternative loss families (cumulative, maximize, smooth saturation) across six configurations; only the MSE variant converged during training (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}).

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn dialogue is the dominant mode of interaction with large language models (LLMs), and system prompts that specify task goals form the foundation of instruction following. As conversations grow longer, however, models progressively lose track of these goal specifications. \citet{dongre2026attention} quantified this degradation across 12 models: the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR), which measures how much attention the model allocates to goal tokens in the system prompt, declines by 27--48\% between early and mid-conversation turns. We replicate and extend this finding on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, observing a 70.8\% relative decline in GAR from 0.0418 at turn~1 to 0.0122 at mid-conversation. We attribute the steeper decline partly to our use of last-token GAR, which captures summary-position attention more sensitively than the all-token average of \citet{dongre2026attention}, and partly to Qwen-2.5-7B's stronger instruction-following fidelity in early turns. This attention decay represents a systematic failure of multi-turn models to maintain task coherence, and it grows worse with each additional turn.

A striking aspect of this degradation is what it does \emph{not} affect. \citet{dongre2026attention} showed that goal-relevant information persists in the residual stream throughout the conversation: linear discriminant analysis probes recover goal content with AUC\,=\,0.98 even at late turns where attention to goal tokens has collapsed. The information is present in the hidden states, but the attention mechanism fails to route it toward the output computation. This dissociation between information availability and information utilization suggests a natural intervention: if the residual stream already carries goal information, a learnable gate on the residual pathway could amplify or attenuate layer contributions to recover goal attention. This reasoning motivates ThreadGate, which modulates each residual update with a content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gate: $x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$, where $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer network conditioned on the hidden state and turn features, inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_overview.pdf}
  \caption{Overview of ThreadGate and its failure mode. (Left) ThreadGate adds per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates to the residual stream, trained jointly with an auxiliary GAR loss that targets goal attention. (Right) Under teacher-forced evaluation, GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants; under autoregressive generation, it drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap. The five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) shows that no intervention provides measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning.}
  \label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

ThreadGate combines per-layer MLP gate networks with a joint training objective: next-token prediction (NTP), an auxiliary GAR loss ($\tau = 0.5$), and a gate diversity loss (\S\ref{sec:method}). The final formulation emerged from seven iterations spanning four loss families; only MSE target with gate diversity converged stably. To diagnose the failure, we construct a five-way controlled comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) where each pair isolates one factor (\S\ref{sec:experiments}). PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} serves a dual role: training strategy and exclusion tool that rules out gradient direction conflict.

Training succeeds: teacher-forced GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants. The five-way comparison reveals that the failure is specific to autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025, a gap exceeding an order of magnitude that persists across all training strategies. All four fine-tuned variants achieve indistinguishable performance (0.75\,pp spread on GAR-ours, identical PPL at 2.78); the gating contrast confirms that gates contribute zero measurable benefit. The PCGrad exclusion experiment eliminates gradient direction conflict without altering any outcome, establishing that conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure. These contrasts converge on exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, where attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). An inference-time steering experiment provides constructive evidence that attention misallocation is a proximal cause: additive attention bias recovers autoregressive GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, bypassing the exposure bias barrier (\S\ref{subsec:steering}). A preliminary scheduled sampling experiment confirms that the gap is also mitigable at training time: GAR-ours increases from 0.016 to 0.318 (20$\times$), compressing the ${\sim}$31$\times$ gap to 1.57$\times$ with no PPL cost (single seed; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Our contributions: (1)~a systematic diagnostic framework for analyzing auxiliary objective failures in multi-turn dialogue, comprising a five-way controlled comparison that isolates four factors (turn conditioning, gating mechanism, gradient conflict, and fine-tuning), PCGrad-based exclusion via gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, and per-layer gradient conflict quantification (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}); (2)~using this framework, the first empirical characterization of how exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention loss generalization (20--33$\times$ gap) while leaving NTP robust, with evidence that token-level self-recovery \citep{he2019exposure} does not extend to auxiliary objectives, a preliminary demonstration that scheduled sampling compresses this gap from ${\sim}$31$\times$ to 1.57$\times$ (single seed), and cross-model confirmation on Llama-3.1-8B (27$\times$ gap); (3)~an inference-time steering experiment: additive attention bias at $\beta$\,=\,5 recovers autoregressive GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, providing constructive evidence that attention misallocation---not representation degradation---is a proximal cause under the tested conditions (Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B, UltraChat, GAR-family losses; \S\ref{subsec:steering}); (4)~a principled negative baseline: the final MSE formulation emerges from six failed loss families, and the five-way comparison rules out four alternative training strategies (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}).

\begin{abstract}
Multi-turn dialogue with Large Language Models (LLMs) suffers from progressive attention decay toward system prompt goal tokens. On Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) declines by 70.8\% between early and mid-conversation turns, yet linear probes recover goal content from the residual stream with AUC\,=\,0.98. We introduce ThreadGate, a per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gate ($x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$) trained jointly with next-token prediction (NTP) and an auxiliary GAR loss. A five-way comparison reveals that training succeeds (teacher-forced GAR\,=\,0.50) but autoregressive GAR drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap; gating contributes zero measurable benefit and a PCGrad-based exclusion experiment rules out gradient conflict. The failure traces to exposure bias: attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text. Inference-time steering ($\beta$\,=\,5) recovers GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, providing constructive evidence that attention misallocation---not representation degradation---is a proximal cause consistent with our evidence. Scheduled sampling compresses the gap to 1.57$\times$ with no PPL cost (single seed), and cross-model evaluation on Llama-3.1-8B confirms the gap (27$\times$ by median). These findings characterize how exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary objective generalization under the tested conditions (Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B, UltraChat, GAR-family losses).
\end{abstract}

\section{Analysis}\label{sec:analysis}

The five-way comparison in \S\ref{sec:experiments} established that gating produces no measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning. This section traces the failure through a three-step diagnostic chain: we first show that gates learn behavior opposite to the design intent (\S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning}), then rule out gradient conflict as the explanation through an exclusion experiment (\S\ref{subsec:pcgrad}), and finally identify distribution shift as the root cause (\S\ref{subsec:dist-shift}). \S\ref{subsec:mechanism-specificity} confirms this selectivity with an orthogonal hidden-state metric, and \S\ref{subsec:cross-model} confirms the failure generalizes across architecturally distinct LLMs.

\subsection{Gate Turn Conditioning}\label{subsec:gate-conditioning}

The design intent of ThreadGate is to modulate the residual stream more aggressively at late conversation turns, where GAR naturally decays (\S\ref{sec:background}). We test whether the learned gates achieve this by comparing gate values at turn~1 (early) and turn~21 (late) across all 28 layers of the trained model.

The gates do the opposite. In 27 of 28 layers, the gate value at turn~1 is lower than at turn~21 (Wilcoxon signed-rank test: $p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$, effect size $r = 0.975$). At layer~23, the gate is 0.769 at turn~1 and 0.979 at turn~21; at layer~14, it is 0.747 at turn~1 and 0.928 at turn~21. The pattern is consistent: gates suppress the residual stream more at early turns (where GAR is already high) and become nearly passive at late turns (where intervention is most needed). This inversion has a straightforward explanation. NTP loss pressure is highest at late turns, where the model must predict tokens conditioned on a long context. The NTP gradient pushes gates toward 1.0 to preserve the full sublayer computation, and this pressure is strongest precisely where GAR-based attenuation would be most useful.

The resulting inference-time gate values range from 0.93 to 0.98 (2--7\% attenuation), far below what meaningful modulation would require; this explains the convergence with LoRA-only in Table~\ref{tab:four-way}. Figure~\ref{fig:gate-shift}(a) visualizes the per-layer gate difference. The inverted turn conditioning raises a natural question: does the NTP-GAR gradient conflict prevent gates from learning the intended behavior? If gradient conflict is the root cause, resolving it should rescue gate conditioning.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_gate_shift.pdf}
  \caption{(a)~Gate turn conditioning: per-layer gate values at turn~1 vs turn~21. In 27/28 layers, gates approach 1.0 at late turns, inverting the design intent ($p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$). (b)~Distribution shift: the 20--33$\times$ GAR gap persists across all five variants (TG, +PCGrad, UG, LoRA-only, Base), the gap persists across all five tested configurations.}
  \label{fig:gate-shift}
\end{figure}

\subsection{PCGrad Gradient Conflict Exclusion}\label{subsec:pcgrad}

The NTP and GAR gradients point in opposing directions across 68--79\% of layers throughout training, with mean cosine similarity ranging from $-$0.089 (epoch~0) to $-$0.042 (epoch~2). The NTP gradient magnitude dominates by 7.5--14.7$\times$. This raises a natural hypothesis: perhaps opposing gradient directions prevent the GAR objective from converging. If so, eliminating direction conflict should prevent the collapse. We test this using PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, which projects the gradient of one task onto the normal plane of the other whenever two task gradients conflict. We apply PCGrad to gate parameters only, scoping our claim to direction conflict; magnitude imbalance \citep{chen2018gradnorm} and Nash equilibrium violations \citep{navon2022nash} remain untested.

Eliminating direction conflict via PCGrad shifts per-step cosine similarity from consistently negative ($-$0.089) to oscillating around zero ($-$0.15 to $+$0.09), yet all outcomes remain unchanged: IID GAR-ours (0.50 vs 0.50), PPL (2.780 vs 2.784), and autoregressive GAR (0.015 vs 0.016) are indistinguishable (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}). $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ follows a nearly identical training trajectory and collapses in the same manner (Figure~\ref{fig:training-pcgrad}b). The exclusion follows by \emph{modus tollens}: if direction conflict causes the failure, removing it should prevent it; PCGrad removes it; the failure persists; therefore direction conflict is not the cause. Direction conflict is neither necessary (removing it does not rescue GAR) nor sufficient ($\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ converges during training despite persistent conflict). The 7.5--14.7$\times$ NTP magnitude dominance renders directional interference inconsequential. With gradient conflict ruled out, the question narrows: the training dynamics are not the problem (GAR converges under teacher forcing), so the failure must occur at the transition from training to inference.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_training_pcgrad.pdf}
  \caption{(a)~Gradient cosine similarity between NTP and GAR objectives. ThreadGate remains consistently negative; +PCGrad oscillates around zero. (b)~IID evaluation outcomes are indistinguishable: eliminating direction conflict changes nothing.}
  \label{fig:training-pcgrad}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Distribution Shift Analysis}\label{subsec:dist-shift}

Gates are ineffective (\S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning}) and gradient conflict is not the cause (\S\ref{subsec:pcgrad}). The TF-AR GAR gap (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}) persists across all training strategies. GAR-Dongre provides a revealing control: under standard training it remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) with no distribution gap, so the shift selectively destroys the concentrated last-token attention pattern while leaving diffuse attention unaffected (scheduled sampling breaks this pattern; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

The mechanism is exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled,arora2022exposure}: during training, the model conditions on ground-truth prefixes; at inference, on its own outputs. The statistical properties diverge, and the 2--7\% gate attenuation (\S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning}) cannot compensate. \citet{he2019exposure} showed that language models exhibit token-level self-recovery from exposure bias, and our PPL results confirm this (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}). The critical distinction is between primary and auxiliary objectives: NTP is robust because it is the same task at both training and inference time, while GAR optimizes a distributional property of the input prefix that has no counterpart under autoregressive generation. Figure~\ref{fig:attention-heatmap} confirms teacher-forced GAR-ours remains near $\tau = 0.5$ (7--8\% decay, $N$\,=\,45), yet autoregressive GAR collapses for all variants (Figure~\ref{fig:gate-shift}b).

\subsection{Mechanism Specificity}\label{subsec:mechanism-specificity}

\S\ref{subsec:dist-shift} showed that exposure bias selectively destroys GAR-ours while GAR-Dongre remains unaffected under standard training. We test whether this selectivity extends beyond attention with Turn-level Residual Coherence (TRC), the cosine similarity between hidden states at the last-token position of adjacent assistant turns. For ThreadGate, TF/AR TRC is 0.334/0.332 (gap $+$0.002); for LoRA-only, 0.349/0.411 (gap $-$0.062, reversed). Neither shows a gap comparable to the GAR collapse, confirming that exposure bias disrupts a specific routing behavior (summary-position attention to goal tokens) while leaving internal representations intact.

Cross-model evaluation extends this selectivity: GAR-Dongre shows negligible distribution shift on both Llama-3.1-8B (AR/TF ratio 1.002$\times$) and Qwen-2.5-7B (1.28$\times$), compared to the order-of-magnitude collapse in GAR-ours (the scheduled sampling experiment in \S\ref{sec:discussion} breaks this pattern, with GAR-Dongre rising to 0.353).

\subsection{Cross-Model Generality}\label{subsec:cross-model}

To test whether the failure generalizes, we replicate on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \citep{grattafiori2024llama3} with Base, ThreadGate, and LoRA-only. Phase~1 diagnostics confirm goal attention decay (GAR: 0.026$\to$0.003 over 10 turns). Teacher-forced GAR-ours ($N$\,=\,50) is 0.484 (TG) and 0.481 (LoRA-only); gating contributes zero benefit. We report median GAR-ours for autoregressive evaluation due to heavy-tailed distributions (5--8\% of turn-level measurements exhibit sporadic attention spikes). Median GAR-ours ($N$\,=\,20 mid-turn conversations, excluding the first two turns) is 0.018 for both TG and LoRA-only (Base: 0.009), yielding TF-AR gaps of 27$\times$ and 26$\times$. The TF-AR gap exceeds 25$\times$ on both architectures, consistent with the exposure bias hypothesis. The gate profile differs---Qwen's minimum at layers~3--4, Llama's at layer~25---yet both converge toward identity under NTP pressure.

\section{Training Hyperparameters}\label{sec:appendix-hyper}

All fine-tuned variants share the following configuration: Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct base model, LoRA rank 16 ($\alpha$=32) on \texttt{q\_proj} and \texttt{v\_proj}, AdamW optimizer (lr = $2 \times 10^{-4}$, $\beta_1$=0.9, $\beta_2$=0.999), batch size 1 with gradient accumulation 8, 3 epochs over 8K UltraChat conversations, maximum sequence length 2048, seed 42. ThreadGate gates use a 10$\times$ learning rate multiplier and initial bias 1.5 ($\sigma(1.5) \approx 0.82$).

\section{Loss Formulation Search}\label{sec:appendix-loss}

Table~\ref{tab:loss-iterations} summarizes the seven loss configurations explored before arriving at the final MSE + gate diversity formulation (\S\ref{sec:loss}). Configurations v1--v6 span three alternative loss families; none converged during training.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\small
\caption{Loss formulation iterations on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. GAR-ours (teacher-forced) and PPL are reported only for the converged configuration.}
\label{tab:loss-iterations}
\begin{tabular}{l l l c c}
\toprule
 & \textbf{Loss Family} & \textbf{Status} & \textbf{GAR} & \textbf{PPL} \\
\midrule
v1  & Cumulative            & \texttimes~GAR loss $\to$ 0     & ---  & ---  \\
v2  & Cumulative + $\varepsilon$ & \texttimes~same trajectory & ---  & ---  \\
v3  & Maximize ($\lambda$=2)  & \texttimes~gate frozen        & ---  & ---  \\
v3b & Maximize ($\lambda$=10) & \texttimes~identity collapse   & ---  & ---  \\
v5  & Maximize ($\lambda$=1)  & \texttimes~identity collapse   & ---  & ---  \\
v6  & Smooth saturation       & \texttimes~identity collapse   & ---  & ---  \\
v7  & MSE + gate div          & \checkmark~converged           & 0.50 & 2.78 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Maximize-family losses ($-\text{mean}(\text{GAR})$) consistently drove gates toward identity: the unbounded objective rewards full pass-through ($g \to 1.0$), collapsing gate diversity (std $<$ 0.025). Bounded variants (smooth saturation) decelerated but did not prevent this collapse, motivating the MSE formulation targeting a finite GAR level.

